# RUNBOOK — <CHART/APP NAME> on Arvan CaaS

## Scope

- Namespace: `<namespace>`
- Release: `<release>`
- Chart version: `<version>`
- OpenAPI baseline: `../references/arvan-caas-openAPI-1.25.json`

## Day-0: Initial install checklist

1) Discovery
- run `verify-cluster.sh` and confirm required APIs/RBAC
- verify namespace quota/limitrange visibility

2) Resource policy
- every workload container has explicit resources
- requests/limits parity enforced for Arvan
- `ephemeral-storage` set when required

3) Secret strategy
- choose one mode:
  - Helm-managed secret creation, or
  - existing secret reference
- confirm `values.secret.yaml` is excluded from git

4) Render and dry run

```bash
bash .codex/skills/caas-arvan-kuber/scripts/render-helm.sh ./helm/<chart> <namespace> values.yaml values.secret.yaml rendered.yaml [values.<env>.yaml]
kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f rendered.yaml
```

5) Deploy and verify readiness

```bash
helm upgrade --install <release> ./helm/<chart> -n <namespace> \
  -f values.yaml -f values.<env>.yaml -f values.secret.yaml \
  --wait --timeout 10m --rollback-on-failure
kubectl -n <namespace> get pods
```

## Day-1: Operations

### Scale stateless workloads

```bash
kubectl -n <namespace> scale deploy/<name> --replicas=3
```

### Scale stateful workloads

- Do not use HPA by default.
- Follow application replication rules first.
- Scale only with explicit operator approval/runbook steps.

```bash
kubectl -n <namespace> scale sts/<name> --replicas=<n>
```

### Rotate app secrets

1) Update `values.secret.yaml` or existing secret.
2) Run Helm upgrade.
3) Verify rollout and app health checks.

### Rotate registry credentials

1) Update dockerconfigjson secret content.
2) Confirm `imagePullSecrets` still references expected secret.
3) Validate new pod pulls succeed.

## Day-2: Maintenance

### Upgrade chart/application

```bash
bash .codex/skills/caas-arvan-kuber/scripts/render-helm.sh ./helm/<chart> <namespace> values.yaml values.secret.yaml rendered.yaml [values.<env>.yaml]
helm upgrade <release> ./helm/<chart> -n <namespace> \
  -f values.yaml -f values.<env>.yaml -f values.secret.yaml \
  --wait --timeout 10m
```

### Rollback

```bash
helm history <release> -n <namespace>
helm rollback <release> <revision> -n <namespace>
```

## Incident playbook

### 1) Helm render/parse failure

Checks:

```bash
helm lint ./helm/<chart> -f values.yaml -f values.secret.yaml
helm template <release> ./helm/<chart> -n <namespace> -f values.yaml -f values.secret.yaml > rendered.yaml
```

Likely causes:

- wrong chart path or missing dependency artifacts
- YAML indentation/quoting issues in templates

### 2) `ImagePullBackOff` / auth pull errors

Checks:

```bash
kubectl -n <namespace> describe pod <pod>
kubectl -n <namespace> get secret <pull-secret> -o yaml
```

Fixes:

- verify secret type is `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson`
- verify pod/serviceaccount `imagePullSecrets` includes the expected secret
- for GitLab Runner, verify executor job pod has `image_pull_secrets`

### 3) RBAC denial (`cannot create secrets/configmaps/pods`)

Checks:

```bash
kubectl -n <namespace> auth can-i create secrets
kubectl -n <namespace> auth can-i create configmaps
kubectl -n <namespace> auth can-i create pods
kubectl -n <namespace> get rolebinding <name> -o yaml
```

Fixes:

- align `RoleBinding` subject namespace with effective service account identity
- verify alias vs canonical namespace identity before broadening permissions
- avoid cluster-scoped RBAC unless explicitly required

### 4) No rollout after successful Helm upgrade

Checks:

```bash
kubectl -n <namespace> rollout status deploy/<name>
kubectl -n <namespace> get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
```

Action:

```bash
kubectl -n <namespace> rollout restart deploy/<name>
```

### 5) Admission deny due to resources

Symptoms:

- rejected ratio/policy checks
- missing resources fields

Fix:

- enforce explicit resources on all containers
- enforce Arvan parity (`requests == limits`)
- add `ephemeral-storage` when policy expects it
